      place: "U.S. Department of Health, Education, and Welfare, Washington, D.C.",
      body: "In July 1973 the Secretary's Advisory Committee on Automated Personal Data Systems, chaired by the computer scientist Willis Ware, published 'Records, Computers and the Rights of Citizens' (DHEW Publication No. (OS) 73-94). It recommended a federal 'Code of Fair Information Practice' for all automated personal data systems, resting on five principles, beginning with 'There must be no personal data record keeping systems whose very existence is secret.' The committee also opposed a standard universal identifier and urged steps to curb the drift of the Social Security number toward becoming one.",
