      phase: "cookies",
      title: "A graduate student finds the governor in 'anonymous' health data",
      place: "Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, Massachusetts",
      body: "Massachusetts' Group Insurance Commission had released patient-level data on about 135,000 state employees and their families, believed to be anonymous, to researchers and sold a copy to industry. Latanya Sweeney, then an MIT graduate student, bought the Cambridge voter registration list for twenty dollars and linked it on ZIP code, birth date and gender. Governor William Weld's record stood out: six Cambridge voters shared his birth date, three were men, and only he lived in his five-digit ZIP code.",
      lat: 42.3601,
      lng: -71.0942,
      continent: "North America",
      source: [
        {
          institution: "International Journal on Uncertainty, Fuzziness and Knowledge-based Systems 10(5), 557-570 (author's copy, Data Privacy Lab)",
          title: "L. Sweeney, 'k-anonymity: a model for protecting privacy'",
          date: "2002",
          url: "https://dataprivacylab.org/dataprivacy/projects/kanonymity/kanonymity.pdf",
          quote: "Governor Weld lived in Cambridge Massachusetts. According to the Cambridge Voter list, six people had his particular birth date; only three of them were men; and, he was the only one in his 5-digit ZIP code.",
          note: "Preceded by: 'For example, William Weld was governor of Massachusetts at that time and his medical records were in the GIC data.' Same page: GIC collected data 'for approximately 135,000 state employees and their families. Because the data were believed to be anonymous, GIC gave a copy of the data to researchers and sold a copy to industry'; 'For twenty dollars I purchased the voter registration list for Cambridge Massachusetts'. The paper's reference for the voter list is dated February 1997.",
          verificationStatus: "CONFIRMED",
          accessType: "FREE"
        },
        {
          institution: "Daniel C. Barth-Jones, Columbia University Mailman School of Public Health (SSRN abstract 2076397; copy hosted by the Future of Privacy Forum)",
          title: "The 'Re-identification' of Governor William Weld's Medical Information: A Critical Re-examination of Health Data Identification Risks and Privacy Protections, Then and Now",
          date: "2012-07",
          url: "https://fpf.org/wp-content/uploads/The-Re-identification-of-Governor-Welds-Medical-Information-Daniel-Barth-Jones.pdf",
          quote: "The 1997 re-identification of Massachusetts Governor William Weld’s medical data within an insurance data set which had been stripped of direct identifiers has had a profound impact on the development of de-identification provisions within the 2003 Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) Privacy Rule.",
          note: "Supports the 1997 date and the HIPAA influence. Barth-Jones also identifies Sweeney as 'then a MIT graduate student in computer science' and argues the match was less certain than usually told because the voter roll covered only about half of Cambridge's population.",
          verificationStatus: "CONFIRMED",
          accessType: "FREE"
        }
      ]
