          url: "https://cyber.harvard.edu/is02/readings/doubleclick.html",
          quote: "On March 2, 2000, Kevin O'Connor, DoubleClick's CEO and Chairman of the Board, announced that he had made a \"mistake\" by planning to merge DoubleClick's and Abacus' databases and stated that DoubleClick would undertake no such merger until it reached an agreement with the United States government and Internet industry regarding privacy standards. … we find that the DoubleClick-affiliated Web sites are \"parties to the communication[s]\" from plaintiffs and have given sufficient consent to DoubleClick to intercept them.",
          note: "Same opinion: 'In June 1999, DoubleClick purchased Abacus Direct Corp. (\"Abacus\") for more than one billion dollars.' and that Abacus held data 'on approximately ninety percent of American households'. The opinion also records that 'The FTC concluded its investigation on January 22, 2001' and quotes the FTC staff closing letter, including 'Specifically, it appears that DoubleClick did not combine PII from Abacus Direct with clickstream collected on client Web sites.' That letter (scanned, no text layer) is at https://www.ftc.gov/sites/default/files/documents/closing_letters/doubleclick-inc./doubleclick.pdf and was read from the page image. Header on the same page: 'March 28, 2001, Decided March 29, 2001, Filed'. The court declined supplemental jurisdiction over the state-law claims.",
