      body: "On 16 April 2002 the W3C issued the Platform for Privacy Preferences 1.0 as a Recommendation, edited by Massimo Marchiori with authors including Lorrie Cranor (AT&T). P3P let a website express its privacy practices in a standard XML vocabulary that user agents could fetch automatically and compare with preferences the user had set, so the user need not read every policy. The specification itself stated that it offered no technical way to make sure sites acted according to their policies.",
