      date: "1604 (Michaelmas term)",
      phase: "castle",
      headline: "The house as castle and fortress",
      narrative: "In Semayne v Gresham the Court of King's Bench resolved that when the Crown is a party a sheriff may break into a house to execute its process, but only after announcing why he has come and asking for the doors to be opened; and that a house is a castle only for its owner, not for others who take refuge in it. Edward Coke's report of the case begins with the resolution that the house of every one is to him as his castle and fortress, for defence and for repose.",
      why: "Turns the home-as-refuge maxim into a working limit on officers of the state — the 'walls' model of privacy in its classic legal form.",
