      contribution: "Kapor, former president of Lotus Development Corporation, co-founded the Electronic Frontier Foundation in 1990 and co-wrote its founding paper with Barlow. In Wired in 1993 he argued that the coming 'information highway' must be built as an open network with published interfaces, and that constitutional protections of privacy should extend to it. He is credited with the maxim 'Architecture is politics', which he dated to 1991.",
      influence: "Lawrence Lessig credited Kapor with giving cyberspace the meme 'Architecture is Politics' and applied it to open-access broadband policy; the idea that design choices settle rights underlies later arguments for privacy by design.",
      thread: "In the same essay Kapor asked that networks let users 'determine the content of the system' and choose their roles 'as consumers, providers, or both' — the person as a party to the network, not only its audience.",
