      contribution: "Zimmermann wrote Pretty Good Privacy, public-key email encryption for ordinary personal computers, and on 5 June 1991 sent the first release to friends who posted it to Peacenet, a Usenet source-code newsgroup and bulletin boards. He said a 1991 Senate anticrime bill urging that communications systems let the government obtain plaintext is what led him to publish it free. PGP 2.0 (September 1992) added what he calls 'the now-famous PGP trust model'; soon after, US Customs opened an export investigation that ran three years and was closed without charges in January 1996.",
      influence: "PGP put strong public-key encryption into the hands of individuals worldwide, and the government's investigation of its spread helped ignite, in Zimmermann's words, 'a controversy that would eventually lead to the demise of the US export restrictions on strong cryptography'.",
      thread: "Zimmermann framed PGP as individual self-help — 'PGP empowers people to take their privacy into their own hands' — and, unlike the rival PEM standard, which required users to 'register a key with something called a policy certification authority', PGP 'requires no bureaucracy' (John Gilmore, 1993).",
      quote: "If privacy is outlawed, only outlaws will have privacy.",
